exercises.reference:
import operator

OPS = {"+": operator.add, "-": operator.sub, "*": operator.mul,
       "/": operator.truediv, "^": operator.pow}

def execute(code, env):
    stack = []
    for instr in code:
        op = instr[0]
        if op == "PUSH":
            stack.append(instr[1])
        elif op == "LOAD":
            name = instr[1]
            if name not in env:
                raise NameError(f"undefined variable {name!r}")
            stack.append(env[name])
        elif op == "STORE":
            env[instr[1]] = stack[-1]
        elif op == "POP":
            stack.pop()
        elif op == "NEG":
            stack.append(-stack.pop())
        elif op in OPS:
            right = stack.pop()
            left = stack.pop()
            stack.append(OPS[op](left, right))
        else:
            raise ValueError(f"unknown instruction {instr!r}")
    return stack[-1]

exercises.solution:
```python
OPS = {"+": operator.add, "-": operator.sub, "*": operator.mul,
       "/": operator.truediv, "^": operator.pow}

def execute(code, env):
    stack = []
    for instr in code:
        op = instr[0]
        if op == "PUSH":
            stack.append(instr[1])
        elif op == "LOAD":
            if instr[1] not in env:
                raise NameError(f"undefined variable {instr[1]!r}")
            stack.append(env[instr[1]])
        elif op == "STORE":
            env[instr[1]] = stack[-1]
        elif op == "POP":
            stack.pop()
        elif op == "NEG":
            stack.append(-stack.pop())
        elif op in OPS:
            right = stack.pop()
            left = stack.pop()
            stack.append(OPS[op](left, right))
        else:
            raise ValueError(f"unknown instruction {instr!r}")
    return stack[-1]
```

The one real trap is operand order. The compiler emits the left operand first, so it is *deeper* in the stack; popping gives the right operand first. Getting this backwards still passes tests with `+` and `*`, which commute, and fails only with `-`, `/` and `^` — a good reminder to test non-commutative cases. Raising on an unknown instruction, rather than ignoring it, turns a compiler bug into an immediate, legible error.

