exercises.reference:
from dataclasses import dataclass

@dataclass
class Num:
    value: float

@dataclass
class Var:
    name: str

@dataclass
class Neg:
    operand: object

@dataclass
class BinOp:
    op: str          # one of + - * / ^
    left: object
    right: object


def compile_expr(node):
    code = []
    _emit(node, code)
    return code

def _emit(node, code):
    if isinstance(node, Num):
        code.append(("PUSH", node.value))
    elif isinstance(node, Var):
        code.append(("LOAD", node.name))
    elif isinstance(node, Neg):
        _emit(node.operand, code)
        code.append(("NEG",))
    elif isinstance(node, BinOp):
        _emit(node.left, code)
        _emit(node.right, code)
        code.append((node.op,))
    else:
        raise TypeError(f"not a Calc expression: {node!r}")

exercises.solution:
```python
def compile_expr(node):
    code = []
    _emit(node, code)
    return code

def _emit(node, code):
    if isinstance(node, Num):
        code.append(("PUSH", node.value))
    elif isinstance(node, Var):
        code.append(("LOAD", node.name))
    elif isinstance(node, Neg):
        _emit(node.operand, code)
        code.append(("NEG",))
    elif isinstance(node, BinOp):
        _emit(node.left, code)
        _emit(node.right, code)
        code.append((node.op,))
    else:
        raise TypeError(f"not a Calc expression: {node!r}")
```

Why does post-order work? By induction on the tree: the code for any subtree, run on any stack, leaves that stack unchanged except for exactly one new value on top — the subtree's value. Numbers and variables push one value. For `BinOp`, the left code pushes the left value, the right code pushes the right value on top of it, and the operator replaces both with the result. Associativity needs no special handling: it is already encoded in the *shape* of the tree the parser built, so `2 ^ (3 ^ 2)` compiles to `PUSH 2, PUSH 3, PUSH 2, ^, ^`.

